pub const LESSON_GOALS : [27]str = [
    "What an FPGA is made of, and why a design uses only part of the chip.",
    "How a chip names itself in 32 bits, and how to read those bits.",
    "How the signals of your design reach the outside world.",
    "How a number is stored in bits, and how balanced ternary stores it in trits.",
    "Why a multiplier is expensive and an adder is cheap, measured on a real chip.",
    "How to read a resource count and find which resource runs out first.",
    "What changes on a clock edge and what does not: combinational wires versus state.",
    "How two clocked blocks agree without dropping a beat: the valid and ready handshake.",
    "How a design of many blocks -- compute, serial, control -- is still one t27 spec.",
    "How a bench is written as data -- clocks, resets, stimulus, checks -- and lowered like any other module.",
    "How a waveform turns a misbehaving design into a named signal at a named cycle.",
    "Why green checks are not proof, and the formal vocabulary for claiming what must hold.",
    "How a t27 function becomes logic, and how a clocked function becomes registers.",
    "How to count what a synthesizer built: LUTs, flip-flops, DSPs, block RAM.",
    "What a 6-input lookup table is, and how 64 bits describe any gate.",
    "How to read a yosys log, and the warnings that become bugs on silicon.",
    "How a synthesizer finds a state machine in your design, or refuses to.",
    "Which part of a design spends the LUTs, and why hierarchy matters.",
    "What place and route does, and where it put a real design.",
    "Why the longest chain of logic sets how fast a design can run.",
    "What slack is, and how to read a timing report path by path.",
    "What FASM is, and how features become configuration frames.",
    "Every stage from a spec to a bitstream, with its time, tool and hash.",
    "What is inside a .bit file, and which chip it was built for.",
    "How a cable finds the chip on a board and reads its IDCODE.",
    "How to show that a bitstream is byte-identical and that it runs.",
    "Put it together: guess what a real t27 module costs in LUTs."
];
pub const LESSON_TEXTS : [27]str = [
    "A CPU runs instructions one after another. An FPGA has no program at all: it is a grid of small blocks -- lookup tables (LUTs), flip-flops, carry chains, block RAM and DSP multipliers -- joined by wires you can switch. Configuring the grid builds a circuit. Our bench chip is an AMD Artix-7 XC7A200T. The skyline draws one routed design on that die: every tile it configures stands up as a column, and most of the chip stays dark.",
    "Before you load anything, you ask the board which chip it has. Over JTAG every chip answers with a 32-bit IDCODE: a version, a part number, the manufacturer and a fixed 1 in bit 0. A bitstream built for the wrong part is refused by the chip, so this is the check that saves you an afternoon. The decoder splits the bits into those four fields and looks the part up in openFPGALoader's own table.",
    "A chip sits in a package: the XC7A200T on our board has 676 solder balls under it. Some carry power or configuration and only some are user IO, grouped into banks with their own voltage. A constraints file (XDC) says which ball each signal of your design uses. Put a signal on a ball that is not user IO and the build fails, or worse, the board does not do what you think.",
    "Hardware stores numbers as fixed groups of bits, and t27 makes you write the width: u8 is 8 unsigned bits, i16 is 16 signed bits. t27 also speaks balanced ternary, where a trit is -1, 0 or +1. For fractions it has GF16, a 16-bit float with 1 sign bit, 6 exponent bits and 9 mantissa bits. Type a number into the calculator and watch how it is rounded, field by field, and how big the error is. The spec in the player names the types a design is built from -- unsigned and signed widths, bool, and clock and reset as kinds of their own -- with a test behind each width.",
    "In logic, every operation has a price paid in chip resources. An adder is a carry chain, cheap and fast. A multiplier built from LUTs is large; the chip has DSP blocks made for multiplying, and a synthesizer uses them when it may. These curves are real yosys counts from 4 to 64 bits. Switch DSPs off and on, and see why hardware people care so much about the width of every number. The spec in the player shows the other bill ternary pays: a word can sit packed into binary words or unpacked, one trit per lane, and the spec models both storages and the add, multiply and dot product on each.",
    "A synthesis report says how many LUTs, flip-flops, DSPs and block RAMs your design takes. The question that matters is which of them runs out first. A design can use few LUTs and still not fit because it needs more DSPs than the chip has. Type a LUT count, or pick one of our real designs, and see how many copies fit on an XC7A200T.",
    "Combinational logic is wires: the output is whatever the inputs are right now, and when the inputs change, the output follows. A register is different: it holds one value and changes it only on a clock edge, so what it holds is a decision the design remembers. Most hardware is these two interleaved: a wall of wires feeding a row of registers feeding the next wall. In t27 the split is written down -- a function named on_clock runs once per edge, and the vars it touches become registers with resets. The player below runs the real t27 compiler in your browser: open clocked_counter.t27 in it and read the Verilog tab, an always block on the edge and the one var of the spec inside it. Under Also try, what a reset does to those registers before any clock arrives.",
    "Two blocks on the same clock still need to agree: one has data, the other has room for it. The agreement is a handshake -- the sender raises valid with the data, the receiver raises ready when it can take it, and the transfer happens on the edge where both are high. Each side is a register and some wires, and the rules hold whether the link carries one byte or a bus channel. The widget shows the compiler own view of a real bus: the AXI4 spec lowered to HIR, its five channels and their handshakes as t27c structures them. Under Also try, the same spec lowered to synthesizable Verilog. The spec in the player is the bus itself -- channel kinds, the widths of address, data and strobe, and the constants that name each channel.",
    "A real design is not one block: a multiply-accumulate core, a UART, control that starts and stops them, resets that reach everything, clocks that feed it all. Spread across files and languages, this is where designs rot -- but the whole of it here is one t27 spec: init, run, stop, the MAC result register, the UART data register, an error flag, and tests over each. The widget shows what the compiler does with such a spec: the whole design lowered to synthesizable Verilog, clocks, resets and bus wiring as it emits them. Count the modules in the Verilog and match them to the spec functions. The buses-and-peripherals course takes the wiring further.",
    "Simulation needs a second design standing over yours: something that drives the inputs, waits, and checks the outputs. That something is the testbench, and here it is not an afterthought in another language -- it is a t27 spec, with the clock period, the reset shape, each stimulus and each expected value written as data, and it lowers to Verilog like any other module. The widget shows a UART testbench the compiler emitted: what a bench looks like when the compiler writes it, with no hand-written Verilog in it. Under Also try, a SPI bench that drives the modes and checks the captures, 7 tests natively. The spec in the player is the vocabulary: clock and reset configs, stimulus, checks with and without a mask.",
    "When a design misbehaves, the waveform is the instrument: every signal, cycle by cycle, so a bug becomes a named wire at a named edge instead of a shrug. The waves below are a real simulation of Verilog generated from a ternary multiply-accumulate spec, driven by that spec's own test vectors -- the same pass marks, drawn as signals. A simulation run is itself something you configure and check. The spec in the player models a simulator's own state: a config with a cycle budget, probes on named signals, a trace written to a VCD file, and results that either pass or hold errors. Under Also try, a viewer for captured VCD files like the one that run writes.",
    "A compiler that accepts your code only tells you the code is well formed. It does not tell you the code means what you meant. The recording shows a tool that found specs which compile, pass every gate and are still read wrongly. For hardware the lesson is sharper: a bug in silicon cannot be patched. The formal vocabulary for the claim is in the spec in the player -- immediate and concurrent assertions, cover points to show a state was reached, assumptions to bound the inputs -- each validated the way the design itself is. The verify-hardware course takes proof from here.",
    "Verilog is the text a synthesizer reads to build a circuit. t27c writes it from the spec. A function named on_clock becomes an always block on the clock edge, and every module var becomes a register with a reset. Open clocked_counter.t27 in the player: an 8-bit counter in a few lines, and the Verilog it turns into. Its header records what yosys built from it.",
    "After synthesis, yosys can print what it built as JSON: how many LUTs, flip-flops, DSPs and block RAMs. Those counts are an honest measure of a design before place and route. Paste a stat -json from yosys into the stat card, or open the real sample, and get a card and a README badge that say plainly that they are a synthesis count. The spec in the player is the thing being counted, in miniature: a ternary full adder with its input table written as tests.",
    "A LUT6 is a tiny memory with 6 address inputs and 64 one-bit entries. Any logic function of 6 inputs is just its truth table written into those 64 bits, the INIT value. Paste an INIT and the widget reads it back as a truth table and an expression. Then play the guessing game on LUTs taken from a real routed design: XOR, AND, a multiplexer, or something with no short name.",
    "yosys tells you a lot, and most of it is fine. A few lines are not: a latch you did not mean, a wire nothing drives, two drivers on one net, a memory that became flip-flops. Each of those works in simulation and fails on the chip. Open one of the real logs from t27 specs, then paste a log of your own.",
    "Many designs are state machines: a register that holds the current state and logic that picks the next one. yosys looks for them and, when it finds one, can re-encode it. When it refuses, it says why. The widget draws the graph yosys extracted from real designs, a UART receiver and a SPI flash master, and shows a case it refused. The spec in the player is the UART behind one of those graphs: ready flags for send and receive, a status byte, and a configure call that sets baud and parity.",
    "A design is built from modules that contain modules. When yosys keeps the hierarchy, it counts each submodule once, and the instance counts tell you how many copies there are. The treemap multiplies them out and shows which submodule eats the LUTs. One adder used many times can cost more than everything else together.",
    "Synthesis says which cells you need. Place and route decides where each one sits on the die and which wires connect them. nextpnr-xilinx does both in the open flow. The map draws every placed cell of a real run on the XC7A200T: a small design is a small cluster in a huge chip.",
    "A signal needs time to pass through each LUT and each wire. The longest chain of logic between two registers sets how fast the clock can tick. yosys can show that chain as a count of cells, before any routing. The staircase draws it for a real GF16 adder, step by step.",
    "Slack is how much time is left on a path when the clock period ends. Positive slack: the path is fast enough. Negative: the design fails at that clock. Each bar here is a real path from a routed design at 50 MHz, split into logic and routing, worst first. Find where the time goes on the worst path. The spec in the player is timing as arithmetic: arcs with delays, a path with slack, a verdict of met or violated.",
    "After routing, the design is a list of features to switch on, in a text format called FASM: this LUT has this INIT, this wire connects to that one. Tools from prjxray turn features into configuration frames, blocks of bits the chip loads. This recording does that step with t27's own tool and compares every frame with the reference tool: they are byte-identical.",
    "A whole build from a t27 spec to an Artix-7 bitstream, written out like a shop receipt: each stage, the tool and its version, the time it took, and the sha256 of what it wrote. No Vivado anywhere. When a number on a receipt can be checked, anyone can rebuild it and compare the hash. The spec in the player is the command that re-runs the bench: what it execs, at which commit, with no help text of its own -- a tool card that says so plainly.",
    "A .bit file is a header followed by packets: commands to the chip's configuration logic and the frames themselves. One of those commands writes the IDCODE of the chip the file was built for. Drop a .bit into the autopsy and see which chip it asks for. Five bitstreams committed to t27 turn out to target a different part than our bench.",
    "JTAG is the cable protocol for talking to the chip on a board. Before programming, the tool shifts out the IDCODE of every device on the chain. This recording decodes the codes our bench answers with, and shows what an even code means: no IDCODE at all. Compare it with the decoder from lesson 2.",
    "The claim most FPGA people doubt: a bitstream built by an open flow from t27 specs, byte-identical to the reference toolchain, and working on a real board. This recording compares the files, loads the bits into the chip's SRAM and counts the receipts the board sends back. Drop two .bit files into bit-diff to check the byte-identical part yourself.",
    "Everything from this course in one game. Each day Gatle shows one real t27 module and the Verilog your browser compiles from it; you have six guesses at how many LUTs yosys spent on it. Use what you know: widths, multipliers, hierarchy, state. Then look at the other ways people and agents compete on t27."
];
pub const LESSON_TASKS : [27]str = [
    "Rotate the die and tap the tallest column: which kind of tile sets the most features? In the spec frame of our bench board, find LUT_TOTAL, then the invariant on DSP48E1_TOTAL: what does its name say our cores use?",
    "Click the bench preset, then flip one bit of the part number and see which device it would be. In the spec frame, the idcode-cfg action names the IDCODE a healthy XC7A100T returns: decode it and compare its part number with the bench chip's.",
    "Search for a ball name like E3, then colour by pin type and find a ball that is not user IO. The spec frame pins a small design to another package, the XC7A100T in CSG324: find the test that puts its clock on ball E3, and the one that counts its pins.",
    "Type 1.618 and read the exponent and mantissa bits; then type 0.1 and look at the error. In the spec frame, find the tests named bool_width and clock_width, and say what width each records.",
    "Pick Multiply, switch DSP48E1 to -nodsp, and compare the LUTs at 32 bits with and without DSPs. In the spec frame, find pack and unpack, and the test that says a fresh hybrid number starts packed.",
    "Pick a real design, then raise the DSP count until something other than LUTs runs out first. In the spec frame, fits_board checks four resources in turn: which one comes last, and how many DSP48 blocks does xc7a100t_resources list?",
    "In the player, switch clocked_counter.t27 to the Verilog tab and find the always block. Then name the one var the spec declares, and say what the test on_clock_increments checks it does.",
    "In the spec frame, find the constants that name the channels and count them. Then find AXI_ADDR_WIDTH and AXI_STRB_WIDTH, and say why a 32-bit data word needs the strobe width it has.",
    "In the spec frame, find start_processing and the test that says it makes the system busy. Then find set_mac_result and get_uart_data, and say which block each belongs to.",
    "In the spec frame, find stimulus and check, then check_with_mask: what does the mask let a test ignore? Find reset_end_cycle, and say when the bench expects the reset to be over.",
    "Zoom into one clock cycle and read the values on the buses at a pass mark. In the spec frame, find sim_config_with_trace and probe: what does the config say the run writes, and what does a ProbePoint name?",
    "In the spec frame, find immediate_assert and concurrent_assert, and say what the concurrent one carries that the immediate one does not. Find with_depth and with_timeout, and say what each one bounds.",
    "Open clocked_counter.t27 in the player and find the always block in its Verilog tab.",
    "Open the real sample, then read the LUT and FF counts on the card. In the spec frame, run the fa tests and read full_adder on the row where all inputs are zero, and the row where all are two.",
    "Try the XOR6 and majority examples, then play five rounds of Guess the gate. In the spec frame, full_adder is built from xor2 and maj3: which one makes the carry, and how many neurons does the spec say it takes?",
    "Open each real log and find the one that yosys refused, and why. In the spec frame, a cross-module pass counts what it cleaned up: find the three counters of CrossOptPass and the test where they add up to 17.",
    "Open the uart_rx sample and count its states and transitions. In the spec frame, find uart_configure and the test that says it changes the baud divisor: what else does one call set besides the divisor?",
    "Switch between the hierarchical and flattened totals and find the largest tile. In the spec frame, find fpga_util and the test that turns 31700 used LUTs into a share of one chip: what percent does it get?",
    "Fit the design, then tap cells of different types and read their BEL names. In the spec frame, find RegionKind: which two of its five kinds are columns, and which test checks that a BRAM column is 1 wide?",
    "Open the real sample and count how many steps of the path are LUTs. In the spec frame, est_comb_delay charges each LUT 100 ps plus 300 ps of routing: find the test that gets 600 for three LUTs.",
    "Click the worst path and compare its logic time with its routing time. In the spec frame, find timing_path with its verdicts is_met and is_violated, then path_delay and lut_delay_ps: how much do three LUT arcs add?",
    "Find where the recording says how many frames matched, out of how many. In the spec frame, HELP_LINE says what tri x7-fasm compares against fasm2frames: of its 20 entries, how many are real and how many must be refused?",
    "Read the receipt: which stage took the longest, and which tool ran it? In the spec frame, read HELP_LINE and ARGS: what one optional argument does tri x7-bench take, and what does DISPATCH say it actually execs?",
    "Open the blink_j26 sample and compare the part in its header with the IDCODE it writes. In the spec frame, read HELP_LINE: against which reference tool is tri x7-writer byte-identical, and what kind of input does it refuse?",
    "Find which IDCODE belongs to an XC7A200T and which to an XC7A100T. In the spec frame, HELP_LINE says how tri fpga-jtag reads a chain with no answer: what does it blame for all ones, and what for all zeros?",
    "Wait for the receipts count at the end, then try bit-diff under Also try. In the spec frame, ACTIONS lists the steps of tri x7-board: which one loads the chip's SRAM, and which flag does HELP_LINE say it needs?",
    "Play today's puzzle. Then read the module's Verilog and say why it cost what it did. Run the tests in the spec frame: how many of the 12 instructions in hello_kernel are GF16 operations, and which test says all 12 take 48 bytes?"
];

; --- Claims --------------------------------------------------------------------------------
test the_course_is_three_cubed {
    assert LESSONS_PER_MODULE == 3;
    assert MODULE_COUNT == 9;
    assert LESSON_COUNT == 27;
    assert MODULE_COUNT * LESSONS_PER_MODULE == LESSON_COUNT;
    assert LESSON_COUNT == 3 * 3 * 3;
}

test it_starts_at_the_chip_and_ends_on_the_board {
    assert MODULE_IDS[0] == "chip";
    assert MODULE_IDS[8] == "board";
    assert LESSON_MODULES[0] == "chip";
    assert LESSON_MODULES[26] == "board";
    assert LESSON_WIDGETS[26] == "gatle";
}

test the_middle_modules_are_hardware {
    assert MODULE_IDS[2] == "clocked-logic";
    assert MODULE_IDS[3] == "proof-in-the-spec";
    assert LESSON_WIDGETS[6] == "play-hello-world";
    assert LESSON_WIDGETS[12] == "play-verilog";
    assert LESSON_SPECS[12] == "specs/ternary/clocked_counter.t27";
}

test every_repointed_lesson_opens_a_hardware_spec {
    assert LESSON_SPECS[3] == "specs/fpga/hw_types.t27";
    assert LESSON_SPECS[4] == "specs/ternary/hybrid_arithmetic.t27";
    assert LESSON_SPECS[6] == "specs/ternary/clocked_counter.t27";
    assert LESSON_SPECS[7] == "specs/fpga/axi4.t27";
    assert LESSON_SPECS[8] == "specs/fpga/top_level.t27";
    assert LESSON_SPECS[9] == "specs/fpga/testbench.t27";
    assert LESSON_SPECS[10] == "specs/fpga/simulator.t27";
    assert LESSON_SPECS[11] == "specs/fpga/formal.t27";
    assert LESSON_SPECS[13] == "specs/ternary/ternary_full_adder.t27";
    assert LESSON_SPECS[16] == "specs/fpga/uart.t27";
    assert LESSON_SPECS[20] == "specs/fpga/timing.t27";
    assert LESSON_SPECS[22] == "specs/tools/trios/tri/x7-bench.t27";
}

test the_course_sends_nothing {
    assert SENDS_NOTHING == true;
    assert LOCALES[0] == "en";
    assert LOCALES[1] == "ru";
}
